module gunbc.plans.ntfy_deployment_gate_approval

// PLAN CARRIER: replacing the GitHub "Review deployments" click with the operator's existing ntfy
// tap, for the fleet-converge jobs that gate behind a deployment environment. Written before any
// implementation PR, because this changes WHAT RELEASES THE srv1 ROOT-MUTATION BOUNDARY — the
// highest-stakes authority question in the repository — and DESIGN.md §3b requires a plan carrier
// naming the invariant, the rejected alternative, and the retirement condition for exactly that
// class of change.
//
// THE OBSERVATION THAT SHAPES THE PLAN (measured, not assumed, on 2026-10-17/18 against
// gunb-ai/gunbc): the operator is NOT currently clicking twice for one decision. `srv1-production`
// is the only environment with RequiredReviewers, it has exactly ONE reviewer (the operator), and
// its gated jobs (dashboard-deploy, approval-broker-dark-install, microvm-controller-install) are
// gated by GitHub ALONE — no ntfy request is filed for them. The two loops already sit on
// DISJOINT jobs: the ntfy loop on gcp-iam-converge and printer (which have no RequiredReviewers at
// all), the GitHub gate on the three srv1 jobs. So the work is not "remove a duplicate authority";
// it is "move the srv1 gate onto the surface the operator already uses". That is a real
// improvement and it is a different, harder thing: it puts a NEW actor in the release path of root
// mutation, and every claim below is written to that bar rather than to the easier story.

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import std.types { List }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanIsAuthorityOnly }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock }
import gunbc.plans.md_helpers { h2, p, li, ul }
import gunbc.design_ruling { project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts }

fn ntfy_deployment_gate_approval_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    p(text: "**Status:** plan, 2026-10-17. Dispatched from work item `adhoc-24134acd-89f`. Design argument plus the operator's UI steps, staged so the GitHub environment settings are touched only by the operator and only after a witnessed approve AND reject on a real dispatch. Authority-only plan: it enforces nothing. The enforcement is the rows it asks for."),

    h2(text: "The ask, and the fact that reframes it"),
    p(text: "The operator: \"recently i'm having to click on github deployments a lot, but i'd rather use nfty.\" One approval surface — the phone tap on the self-hosted ntfy loop — with the GitHub \"Review deployments\" click gone for gated fleet-converge jobs."),
    p(text: "The dispatch's premise was that jobs sit behind BOTH the ntfy loop and RequiredReviewers, so the operator clicks twice for one decision, which §3 would forbid. **Live measurement says otherwise, and the plan is built on the measurement.**"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "GET /repos/gunb-ai/gunbc/environments returns exactly three environments — gcp-iam-converge, mtcollins1-boot, srv1-production — with total_count 3. The names mtjade1-arrival, namecheap-dns and printer-lan appear as `environment:` keys in fleet-converge.yml (lines 3289, 3167, 3413) but do not exist on the repository; those jobs' environment references are unresolvable. GET /repos/gunb-ai/gunbc/deployments?environment=<name> returns zero deployments for all three, so they were never created implicitly either — a job that deploys to a named environment causes GitHub to create it, and these never did."),
      li(text: "Only srv1-production has protection at all: protection_rules = [required_reviewers, branch_policy] and deployment_branch_policy \{protected_branches:false, custom_branch_policies:true\}, with exactly one branch rule, named `main`. gcp-iam-converge and mtcollins1-boot have protection_rules = [] and no branch policy."),
      li(text: "srv1-production's single required reviewer is mtcollins1 (the operator), type User."),
      li(text: "The ntfy loop is filed by exactly three steps, all in fleet-converge.yml: gcp-iam-converge's `gcp_iam_request` step (step at :3103, submission at :3116) and `branch_run_grant_request` (:3121), and printer's observe-or-deliver step (:3518). Every one of them files under the ONE authority the broker brokers — approval_capability_authority = \"gunbc.auth.scoped_authorization\" (approval_decision_store.dag:135). There is no per-job authority string; my earlier draft named one that does not exist. No environment except srv1-production has RequiredReviewers, so none of these three double-gates."),
      li(text: "The three srv1-gated jobs file NO ntfy request. dashboard-deploy (:2469, environment at :2472), approval-broker-dark-install (:2602/:2605) and microvm-controller-install (:2700/:2703) are gated by GitHub alone; only the submission MAC key (approval-submission-mac-key/versions/1, :1658–:1661) is materialized on the broker host, and nothing on that path files or waits for a request. So the double-authority the dispatch describes exists on NO job today: this plan ADDS the ntfy loop to jobs that never had it, and then removes GitHub's. That is the work; it is not the cleanup of a pre-existing duplicate, and the operator should hear it that way."),
      li(text: "The dispatch cited run 35447067113 as \"one phone tap, bound to one request revision\". The run exists — a fleet-converge dispatch created 2026-09-19T13:53:01Z, conclusion failure — and it did carry the srv1-gated dashboard-deploy job. The tap itself is not verifiable from here: broker and capability state live on the tailnet-only broker and were not read. So the ntfy loop is verified as real and executed for the two authorities that file it, and the specific single-tap account for that run is taken as reported rather than confirmed."),
    ]),
    p(text: "So there is no duplicate authority to delete. The claim \"one human decision, two enforcement points\" is what makes this worth doing at all, and it is TRUE OF THE END STATE, not the present one: today those three jobs are gated once, by GitHub, by a click the operator does not want to make. Moving them onto ntfy replaces that authority; it does not reduce the number of authorities. Everything below is written for that bar."),

    h2(text: "What is kept, unchanged"),
    p(text: "Both real environments and their policies stay, because both are load-bearing boundaries rather than conveniences:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "srv1-production's protection IS the boundary for srv1 root mutation (gunbc.auth.github_deployment_environment), and its main-only deployment-branch rule is what stops a non-main ref from mutating srv1 as root. It is reproduced verbatim in the read obligation on srv1_production_required_protection."),
      li(text: "mtcollins1-boot is named by the boot-federation row whose WIF attributeCondition requires `assertion.environment == 'mtcollins1-boot'` (gunbc.auth.managed_host_boot_federation, via claim_pins_attribute_condition over fleet_converge_job_claim_pins). Deleting the environment, or dropping its branch policy, would silently un-federate host boot."),
      li(text: "The main-only branch rule stays on srv1-production whatever answers the review. A protection RULE and a BRANCH POLICY are orthogonal in GitHub's model (branch_policy is one of the three protection_rules types); replacing the reviewers does not touch it, and the plan requires it to be re-read after the switch rather than assumed intact."),
    ]),
    p(text: "can_admins_bypass is currently true on all three environments. That is not changed by this plan, but it is recorded here because it bounds what the new rule proves: with bypass on, an admin can walk past the gate the broker is enforcing, so the rule is not by itself the boundary."),

    h2(text: "The selected pattern, and why it is the only one that can work"),
    p(text: "The pattern goes through gunbc.auth.authorization_pattern_selection, not around it (§3d): the question is which authorization pattern performs the privileged effect \"let this run proceed into srv1 as root\"."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "The GitHub-side reviewer must be a GITHUB APP. GitHub's own REST reference is explicit: \"GitHub Apps can only review their own custom deployment protection rules. To approve or reject pending deployments that are waiting for review from a specific person or team, see POST /repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/actions/runs/\{run_id\}/pending_deployments.\" The fine-grained requirement for POST .../deployment_protection_rule is the \"Deployments\" repository permission, write, on an installation access token. A human-PAT or operator-token review call is therefore excluded twice over: by the endpoint's own App-only contract for App-published rules, and by authorization_pattern_selection's hard constraint against an operator credential where a workflow or service can reach it."),
      li(text: "The App answers ONLY after redeeming a capability the operator approved by ntfy tap, bound to that run id, that environment and that request revision. It cannot answer on its own initiative, which is what keeps it an actuator rather than a second authority."),
      li(text: "The operator is removed from the required-reviewer list only AFTER the rule is live and has been witnessed approving AND rejecting a real dispatch — so there is never a moment where srv1 root mutation is gated by neither."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Rejected alternatives (recorded so the next author does not re-derive them)"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Notify-only ntfy with a link into GitHub. Rejected: the click survives, and the click is the ask."),
      li(text: "The broker holding the operator's own GitHub token to call POST .../pending_deployments. Rejected: it puts an operator credential where a workflow or service reaches it, which authorization_pattern_selection excludes as a hard constraint; and it makes the broker impersonate the operator rather than act as the broker, so the decision and its execution share one identity."),
      li(text: "Deleting the environment or its branch policy. Rejected: breaks the boot-federation WIF condition and the srv1 boundary. Not offered as a simplification."),
      li(text: "A public webhook from GitHub to srv1. Rejected as the default discovery path: the broker is tailnet-only and the endpoint is documented loopback-bound plus tailscale-served with an mTLS principal admission. Publicly exposing the broker to receive GitHub's deployment_protection_rule webhook is a new exposure for the approval authority itself, and the plan does not need it (below)."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "How the broker learns a run is waiting — the open question, answered with citations"),
    p(text: "GitHub's custom protection rule fires a deployment_protection_rule webhook to the App's configured webhook URL. The broker cannot receive it publicly. Two facts from the upstream settle whether that matters:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "The review call is a plain REST call with the App's installation token, bound to the run: POST /repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/actions/runs/\{run_id\}/deployment_protection_rule with body \{environment_name, state: approved|rejected, comment?\} and 204 on success. Nothing in the reference makes the call contingent on a webhook having been delivered; the docs' own worked example is a manual curl of that POST after generating an installation token, and the fine-grained note lists only \"GitHub App installation access tokens\" as an accepted token type."),
      li(text: "GET /repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/actions/runs/\{run_id\}/pending_deployments is documented as \"Get all deployment environments for a workflow run that are waiting for protection rules to pass\", usable by anyone with read access. It carries wait_timer, wait_timer_started_at, current_user_can_approve and reviewers — enough to tell that a job is waiting and on whom."),
      li(text: "A TRIGGER THAT DECIDES THE QUESTION: the same REST page states \"GitHub Apps can only review their own custom deployment protection rules. To approve or reject pending deployments that are waiting for review from a specific person or team, see POST .../pending_deployments.\" So the App can never answer a required-reviewer wait. Polling pending_deployments therefore cannot REPLACE the human gate — it can only observe it. A broker that polls, sees a wait, and calls the review endpoint against a required-reviewers environment is aiming at a gate it does not own. Discovery must come from the run that needs answering, and the poll is kept only as read-only liveness evidence."),
      li(text: "A triggered rule waits up to 30 days for a response before it times out and the workflow job FAILS. So a broker that is down, a lost request, or an App call that never lands leaves the deployment waiting and eventually failing — never approved. Fail-closed is GitHub's default here, not something the broker must implement. The honest cost is that fail-closed is not prompt: a dead broker stalls a deploy for up to a month."),
    ]),
    p(text: "Selected discovery: the run's OWN gated job files the approval request, then waits. This is the shape the repository already executes — gcp-iam-converge's gcp_iam_request step files a stored request and polls the broker to a terminal state before doing any work — and it requires no new inbound exposure. GitHub never contacts srv1; srv1's broker contacts GitHub outbound with an installation token. The webhook remains the upstream's mechanism for the App, and is modelled, but this design does not depend on its delivery: the poll and the review call are both broker-initiated. The alternative (polling waiting runs from the broker with a repo-scoped token) is kept as the fallback and is recorded as weaker, because the broker would then be guessing which run to attribute a tap to instead of being told by the run that needs it."),

    h2(text: "The capability binding, and the red that proves it bites"),
    p(text: "gunbc.auth.approval_capability binds a capability to authority, subject and scope, with a revision and a single-use consumption record. The review call adds two binding facts that must be CHECKED AGAINST GITHUB, not trusted from the request: the workflow run id, and the environment name. The broker posts its review only at the URL for the run it verified, and only for the environment the capability names."),
    p(text: "The load-bearing danger is a request that names run A while the human's tap was for run B, or names gcp-iam-converge while srv1-production is waiting. Note the asymmetry the model now pins down: the RUN ID travels in the URL path, but the ENVIRONMENT travels in the request BODY as environment_name — it is not in the path at all. So a review aimed at the wrong environment is invisible to any check that validates the URL, and only a check on the body can catch it. The discriminating red for the implementation PR is therefore specific and must mutate the body: a capability binding with a wrong run id, OR a body whose environment_name is not the environment the capability names, must NOT approve. A broker that approves anyway is the failure this whole feature could cause, since srv1 root mutation is what sits behind the gate."),
    p(text: "The inhabitance claim is on the real route: POST /repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/actions/runs/\{run_id\}/deployment_protection_rule reached by the broker's own client and witnessed with a 204, not a stand-in URL or a hand-built request. The witness must observe the response code from the real call, because 204 is the only success signal GitHub gives — there is no body to inspect, so nothing else can distinguish \"review accepted\" from \"request rejected\"."),

    h2(text: "Fail-closed arms (each must leave the deployment WAITING or REJECTED, never approved)"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Broker down / unreachable: the gated job's poll times out and the step fails; if it had proceeded it would still be waiting, and at 30 days GitHub fails the job. No approval."),
      li(text: "Operator rejects the tap: the broker posts state=rejected (which must be a real call, witnessed), and the job does not proceed. A rejection that fails to post leaves it waiting — the same terminal outcome for the operator, and the audit distinguishes them."),
      li(text: "Capability expired: redemption is refused, so no review is posted. Waiting, then job failure."),
      li(text: "Revision mismatch (a re-filed request after a change of plan): the broker must refuse rather than answer the stale revision — the same rule the existing loop already enforces (\"An earlier revision cannot clear a pending request created after it\")."),
      li(text: "App call fails (403 missing Deployments:write, 404, rate limit, network): the broker must NOT treat a non-204 as success, must not retry into an approval, and must surface the failure to the same terminal path as a refusal. Since 204 is the only success signal, \"we got 2xx-ish and moved on\" is not admissible."),
      li(text: "App identity revoked or its installation uninstalled: same shape as the call failing. The gate stays shut."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Which GitHub App carries Deployments:write — the second open question"),
    p(text: "extdeps.github.app models GitHubAppPermissionAxis::Deployments already, and gunbc.auth.github_apps models the declared App identities (gunbai-bot and gunbai-ci) as operator-registrations. No existing declared identity is known to carry Deployments:write, and the review endpoint requires it, so this is a registration change rather than a new invention. The decision to put to the operator: extend an existing declared identity, or register a purpose-built broker App."),
    p(text: "The recommendation is a SEPARATE, least-privilege App with exactly the permission set the upstream asks for — Actions read-only and Deployments read and write, subscribed to the deployment_protection_rule event — installed on the one repository, with no other broker duty sharing its credential. Reasons, all §2/§3: the review call is the App's entire job, so its permission set and its blast radius are the same size; the heal publisher and the CI App each already carry their own permission sets for their own subjects, and widening one of those to reach srv1 deployment review would give one App two unrelated authorities over the repository; and least-privilege is what makes the fail-closed arms cheap — revoking this App removes only the ability to answer a rule nobody else published."),
    p(text: "This is not a decision to make quietly: whatever App it is, its installation token is the credential that releases srv1 root mutation once a human has tapped. Its private key custody must follow the same host-custody rule as the broker's own secrets (gunbc.host.host_secret_custody), not a workflow secret."),

    h2(text: "Modelling obligations that must land before any wiring"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "extdeps.github.deployment_protection_rule (first PR): the deployment_protection_rule webhook payload and its single `requested` activity; the review endpoint path/method/required body fields/204; the two accepted states; the pending-deployments poll and its field names; the fact that the environment projection's protection_rules type enum carries only required_reviewers, wait_timer and branch_policy (an enabled custom rule is NOT a fourth type value — inventing one would be a fabricated field); the Deployments-write requirement; and the 30-day timeout-then-fail. Each cited, and each with a fn whose test refuses a mutation of the fact."),
      li(text: "The App's creation, its installation, the webhook URL configuration, and enabling the rule on srv1-production are operator UI acts, and they are modelled as std.human_intervention rows with frequency FleetOnce — not as prose in a runbook — so the human burden of this design is countable next to the others in the provisioning fleet."),
      li(text: "authorization_pattern_selection gains a row for the effect \"answer a pending environment gate for a run\", recording the selected pattern (App-published custom rule answered by the broker after capability redemption) and the excluded patterns (operator token; human pending_deployments call by the broker; notify-only)."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Stages, and the gate between them"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Stage 1 — model (PR 1, no behaviour change): the extdeps module, rostered in scope_carrier_paths with its extdeps_model_scope, plus a witness in dag/test/claim whose reds mutate the upstream facts (a review state outside the enum, a collapsed endpoint pair, an invented protection-rule type, a timeout read as approval)."),
      li(text: "Stage 2 — broker review (PR 2): the binding check and the review call, with the discriminating red on a mutated run id / environment. Inhabitance on the real route. Dark: it must not be reachable for srv1-production while RequiredReviewers still stands."),
      li(text: "Stage 3 — enable (operator): create/install the App, configure its webhook, enable the rule on srv1-production. Nothing in this stage is done by an agent; those are the human_intervention rows."),
      li(text: "Stage 4 — witness (the gate): on a real dispatch, witness the rule APPROVE after a tap and REJECT after a refusal, against a non-mutating target. The plan does not let stage 5 start on a green unit test; it requires both outcomes observed on GitHub."),
      li(text: "Stage 5 — remove the human reviewer (operator), only after stage 4. Then re-read GET .../environments/srv1-production to confirm the branch policy still shows exactly the one main rule — the removal must not have taken the boundary with it."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "What this plan does NOT claim"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "It does not claim the broker's ntfy path is currently wired for srv1-production, or that the fleet-converge workflow files a request there today. It does not today."),
      li(text: "It does not claim the three jobs are safe to gate by rule rather than reviewer. The proof of that is stage 4, which has not happened."),
      li(text: "It does not claim the unresolvable environment references (mtjade1-arrival, namecheap-dns, printer-lan) are unrelated to this work. They are a live defect worth its own item: three fleet-converge jobs name environments the repository does not have, so those jobs are gated by nothing. Reporting it is in scope; fixing it is not, and folding it in would blur which change moved the srv1 boundary."),
      li(text: "It does not claim can_admins_bypass should stay true. It flags that with bypass on, the new rule is not the whole boundary, and leaves that call to the operator."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Retirement"),
    p(text: "This carrier dissolves when the rows it asks for exist and enforce what they say: the extdeps module is rostered and cited; authorization_pattern_selection carries the selected and excluded patterns for the effect; the human_intervention rows exist for the App registration, installation and rule enablement; the broker's review call is witnessed with a 204 on the real route for both approved and rejected on a real dispatch; the srv1-production branch policy has been re-read post-removal; and a mutated binding has been shown not to approve. Until then this is the only place the measured state, the rejected alternatives and the stage gate are written down, and deleting it would cost the next author the same measurements."),
  ]
}

data ntfy_deployment_gate_approval_plan: Plan = Plan {
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly { ruling: project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts },
  slug: "ntfy-deployment-gate-approval",
  title: "Approve GitHub deployment gates from ntfy: what actually gates srv1 today, and the staged swap that keeps the boundary closed",
  body: ntfy_deployment_gate_approval_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen {
    condition: unbound_dissolution(
      description: "Delete this record when the rows it asks for are in the tree and enforce what they claim: extdeps.github.deployment_protection_rule is rostered and cited with its discriminating witness green by execution; gunbc.auth.authorization_pattern_selection carries the selected App-rule pattern and the excluded operator-token / broker-calls-human-reviewer / notify-only patterns for the effect 'answer a pending environment gate for a run'; std.human_intervention FleetOnce rows exist for the App creation, installation and per-environment rule enablement; the broker's review call is witnessed with 204 on the real route for BOTH approved and rejected on a real dispatch; srv1-production's main-only branch policy has been re-read after the required-reviewer removal; and the mutated-binding red (wrong run id, wrong environment) is shown not to approve. Until every one holds, this carrier is the only place the measured gate state, the rejected alternatives and the stage gate are written down.",
    ),
  },
}
